dsh: reasoning:
Interesting. Note the F0 taxonomy line 94 says the variant SET is "Strictly stronger than the parent class" while F1's rev13 corrected it to "strictly WEAKER". That's a live inconsistency between F0 canonical taxonomy (hash 0abb9ed8a961, G-F0 passed!) and F1 (d9cebb9404b2). Wait — let me re-read.

F0 line 94: "Strictly stronger than the parent class: gamma outside the union implies no single q sees a tail of gamma, but not conversely."

F1 line 235: "strictly WEAKER than this class's single-q tail predicate: the single-q tail predicate entails the union reading, and non-containment in the union implies no single q sees a tail of gamma, but not conversely".

Hmm — these two claims are contradictory in wording! F1 says single-q entails union (so union is weaker — i.e., more geodesics are visible under the union reading, so the WCC conclusion "no visible singularity" is *harder* to satisfy under the union reading... wait let's be careful).

Actually: predicate V_singleq(γ) = ∃q∈I+, ∃t0: tail ⊂ J⁻(q). Predicate V_union(γ) = γ([0,T)) ⊂ ⋃_{q∈I+} J⁻(q).

If V_singleq(γ) ⟹ V_union(γ), then V_union is weaker as a *predicate on γ* (holds for more γ). Then "visible_singularity_exists" = ∃γ V(γ) is a weaker (easier) statement under V_union. And the WCC conclusion (no visible singularity) is stronger under V_union. So a class with the union predicate asserts a stronger conclusion but... hmm, what does "strictly stronger class" mean? F0 says variant SET is "strictly stronger than the parent class." F1 says the variant SET relation is "strictly WEAKER than this class's single-q tail predicate" (about the predicate, specifically "non-containment in the union implies no single q sees a tail of gamma, but not conversely").

Hmm, wait: "the single-q tail predicate entails the union reading" — V_singleq ⟹ V_union. OK so union predicate is weaker as a predicate. F0's text says "Strictly stronger than the parent class: gamma outside the union implies no single q sees a tail of gamma, but not conversely." — "gamma outside the union implies no single q sees a tail" is the same direction (¬V_union ⟹ ¬V_singleq, i.e. V_singleq ⟹ V_union). So the entailment direction is the same in both texts! The difference is only whether you call the variant "stronger" or "weaker". Under a predicate reading, V_union is weaker. But as a *class/statement* — the class asserts "no γ with V(γ) exists" (WCC). If V_union is weaker as a predicate, the statement "no γ satisfies V_union" is stronger as a statement. So F0's "strictly stronger than the parent class" can be read as "the class statement is stronger" and F1's "strictly WEAKER than this class's single-q tail predicate" reads as the predicate being weaker. Both could be right under different readings — a wording/framing divergence, not a mathematical one. But note F0 rev has "corrected" direction only in F1's rev13; F0 still says "strictly stronger". Hmm, and the map's direction says F1's rev13 corrected it. But the F0 taxonomy (canonical, gated G-F0 pass at 0abb9ed8a961) retains "Strictly stronger than the parent class". This is a *downstream consistency* issue: F1's rev13 delta says "the variant SET relation is corrected from 'strictly STRONGER' to 'strictly WEAKER'" — but it's corrected only in F1, not in F0. The F0/F1 consistency check (`f0_binding.consistency_evidence_sha256`) may not cover this.

Hmm, but is this genuinely a contradiction, or a difference in what's being compared ("class" vs "predicate")? This could be a good bounded task: check the F0↔F1 variant-SET direction statement for consistency, i.e., whether the F0 canonical taxonomy's variant SET clause ("strictly stronger than the parent class") is consistent with F1's rev13 clause ("strictly WEAKER than this class's single-q tail predicate"). If the two artifacts express opposite relations over the same objects, then either F0 must be amended (hash change, G-F0 verdict void) or the F1 text must be read as predicate-vs-statement.

Hold on. Was the F0 "strictly stronger" already flagged? Let me check whether F0 taxonomy was updated later. G-F0 passed at 0abb9ed8a961 at 01:09:51 — but publication_status says canonical_sha256 = 0abb9ed8a96135c9... And the F1 rev13 delta happened at 00:53:20, before G-F0 pass. So F0 still says "strictly stronger". Let me verify the current F0 file hash and text (maybe it was updated at 01:09 — the research_map/formulation_taxonomy.yaml mtime? publication checked at 01:12:39).

Wait, do the F0 and F1 sentences actually conflict? Let me parse precisely:

F0 (variant definition): "replaces the single-q tail predicate by the set-based condition... Strictly stronger than the parent class: gamma outside the union implies no single q sees a tail of gamma, but not conversely."

So F0 explicitly calls variant SET "strictly stronger than the parent class" and gives the entailment justification. F1 calls the variant SET "strictly WEAKER than this class's single-q tail predicate" with the same entailment facts. This is a direct wording contradiction: "strictly stronger" vs "strictly weaker" — unless you interpret "stronger/weaker" differently (class strength vs predicate strength). But a class is defined by its predicate; confusing.

Also F1's variant relation text says "the single-q tail predicate entails the union reading" and F0 says the same. So the *mathematics* agrees; the *adjective* disagrees. That's a real freeze-completeness/consistency finding: two canonical artifacts that must be consistent disagree on the direction label for the same variant. Under PROTOCOL rule and CF-4 policy ("statement changes need a named lead decision"), a checker should flag it, not fix it.

